In this challenge, the target stopped leaving `read` permissions on `/flag`. Instead, they set up a `flag_checker` binary that asks them to type the flag every time they log in. 

Since we have write access to their `.bashrc`, the goal is to interrupt that login check and grab the flag when they type it.

What Didn't Work: `$PATH` Hijacking

My initial plan was to override the victim's `$PATH` inside `.bashrc` so it pointed to a fake `flag_checker` binary sitting in my home directory. 

That immediately hit an error:
this file is not owned by root

The system checks binary ownership, so pointing $PATH to an unprivileged binary in my user directory was a dead end.

What Did Work: Writing in Bash Function 

The trick here comes down to how Bash resolves commands. Before Bash even looks at $PATH to find a binary, it checks for active shell functions.

Since functions take priority, we don't need a binary on disk. We can just append a custom function named flag_checker directly to their .bashrc after setting their original PATH to NULL:
flag_checker() {
    echo "Type the flag"
    read flag
    echo $flag
}
When the victim logs in and the script calls flag_checker, Bash executes our shell function instead of searching $PATH for the real executable. It prompts them for the flag, grabs the input, and spits it right back out to us.
